<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" />
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Which llama.cpp build gets compiled in. Exactly one, because both packages publish a libllama
    for the same RID and having two would make which one loads a matter of file ordering.

    The default is named "Cpu" and that name is misleading on a Mac: this package is what carries
    libggml-metal.dylib for osx-arm64, so it is the Metal backend there and the CPU backend
    everywhere else. It is the right default on every platform that has no discrete GPU.

    CUDA is opt-in at build time rather than detected, and that follows from D28: Engram is built
    on the machine that will run it, so the person running the build is the one who knows whether
    this box has an NVIDIA card and a driver to match. Detecting it would mean probing for a
    driver at build time and guessing wrong on a build host that differs from the target.

        dotnet publish src/Engram.Cli -c Release -r linux-x64 -p:EngramGpu=cuda12

    What is verified here and what is not. A linux-x64 cuda12 build was run and its output
    inspected, so the packaging is measured: LLamaSharp.Backend.Cuda12 is a metapackage over
    .Cuda12.Linux and .Cuda12.Windows, and the natives land in runtimes/<rid>/native/cuda12/ beside
    the CPU package's runtimes/<rid>/native/{noavx,avx,avx2,avx512}/. Which of those loads is
    LLamaSharp's decision, not Engram's — see LlamaNative for why nothing here tries to make it.
    What is not measured is a CUDA device executing, because this repo's Mac has none. If it does
    not load, the vector lane degrades to lexical with llama.cpp's own reason attached, and
    provider = "openai-compat" against a runtime you start yourself remains available.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp.Backend.Cpu" Condition="'$(EngramGpu)' == ''" />
    <PackageReference Include="LLamaSharp.Backend.Cuda12" Condition="'$(EngramGpu)' == 'cuda12'" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The schema doc is compiled in rather than copied into source, so
         docs/engram-schema.sql remains the single authority for database shape and
         cannot drift from what the binary actually applies. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="../../docs/engram-schema.sql" LogicalName="Engram.Core.Schema.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
